5. Component Architecture
5.1 Interpreter Layer
Purpose: Converts natural language requests or form inputs into typed workflow specifications.
Classes:
	•	NLInterpreter → parses utterances, produces DslSpec.  
	•	DslSpec → typed object defining workflow type, inputs, and desired outputs.  
	•	YamlWorkflowBuilder → constructs YAML DAGs from DslSpec.  
Design Rationale:
	•	The DSL layer enables safe interpretation of business intent into deterministic workflow configs.  
	•	Prevents direct LLM invocation, avoiding “black box” behavior.  
	•	YAML allows versioning, review, and rollback — critical for compliance validation.  



5.2 Configuration Layer
Purpose: Houses YAML workflow definitions that represent a Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG) of executable nodes.
Example YAML:
workflow:
  id: covenant_breach_check
  nodes:
    - id: fetch_feed
      type: tool
      port: sql.query
      params:
        query: SELECT * FROM covenants WHERE breached=TRUE
    - id: assess
      type: capability
      port: nlp.classify
      params:
        rubric: breach_severity
    - id: approve
      type: human_gate
      approvers: [credit_risk_officer]
      timeout: 24h

Rationale:
	•	YAML is human-readable, auditable, and diff-friendly for regulatory review.  
	•	YAML also supports conditional logic, branching, and node chaining via declarative syntax.  



5.3 Orchestration Service
Purpose: Executes YAML workflows using a state machine and command pattern.
Classes:
	•	OrchestratorService  
	•	WorkflowStateMachine  
	•	Node (abstract)  
	◦	CapabilityNode  
	◦	WorkerNode  
	◦	RouterNode  
	◦	HumanGateNode  
	◦	CriticNode  
Execution Flow:
	1	OrchestratorService.run() loads YAML → builds DAG.  
	2	The state machine iterates nodes by dependency.  
	3	Each node triggers a StepCommand.  
	4	The command uses CapabilityInvoker to execute the node safely.  
	5	Results are persisted and logged at each stage.  
Rationale:
	•	Decouples orchestration logic from execution logic.  
	•	Enables resumable workflows and transactional rollback — critical for banking workflows like loan onboarding.  



5.4 Capability Engine
Purpose: Execute domain-specific AI/ML logic (classification, regression, forecasting).
Interfaces:
	•	CapabilityPort → base interface.  
	•	TextGenPort, RegressPort, ForecastPort, etc.  
Adapters:
	•	AzureOpenAITextGenAdapter  
	•	AMLSklearnAdapter  
	•	LocalLLMAdapter  
Design Rationale:
	•	Abstract ports isolate business logic from model providers.  
	•	Supports multi-model, multi-cloud deployment with no code changes.  
	•	Each adapter is registered dynamically via YAML binding.  
Example binding:
bindings:
  regress:
    - provider: AML
      class: AMLRegressAdapter
      constraints:
        env: prod

5.5 Governance Layer (Interceptors)
Purpose: Enforce compliance and operational policies transparently.
Classes:
	•	Interceptor (abstract)  
	•	PiiRlsInterceptor → masks PII, enforces Row-Level Security.  
	•	BudgetInterceptor → enforces token/$ budget limits.  
	•	RetryInterceptor → handles transient API failures.  
	•	TelemetryInterceptor → collects run metrics.  
Pattern: Decorator / Proxy
Each call passes through an interceptor chain before execution.
Rationale:
	•	Ensures uniform enforcement of policies across all capabilities.  
	•	Enables runtime compliance without recompilation.  



5.6 Persistence & Observability
Repositories:
	•	RunStateRepository — current node and state tracking (Cosmos DB).  
	•	AuditRepository — immutable log of actor, action, latency, and cost (Postgres).  
	•	ArtifactRepository — storage of generated outputs (Blob Storage).  
Telemetry:
	•	Each node emits metrics: latency, cost, model version, provider.  
	•	Aggregated into dashboards for business insight and anomaly detection.  
Rationale:
	•	Unified audit schema simplifies regulatory reporting.  
	•	Run metadata supports root cause analysis and optimization.  



5.7 Registry & Strategy Layer
Classes:
	•	CapabilityRegistry — factory for adapter creation.  
	•	ProviderBinding — configuration schema for adapters.  
	•	ProviderStrategySelector — chooses best-fit provider at runtime.  
Example:
If Azure OpenAI is rate-limited, the selector reroutes to LocalLLM automatically.
Rationale:
	•	Maintains runtime continuity.  
	•	Prevents vendor outages or pricing changes from halting workflows.  



