13. Technical Glossary & Design Pattern Definitions

Term / Pattern
Definition (Plain English)
Why It Matters Here
Abstract Base Class (ABC)
A class that defines what must be implemented, not how. Like a job description for subclasses.
All AI capabilities (regress, classify, forecast) follow the same template, ensuring uniformity and extensibility.
Interface / Port
A contract defining allowed actions and data types.
Decouples the system from specific model or vendor APIs.
Adapter
A wrapper that translates a specific model or API to match a common interface.
Allows switching from Azure OpenAI to AWS Bedrock or Local LLM without breaking logic.
Factory Pattern
Centralized class that creates objects based on configuration.
Enables dynamic creation of model providers at runtime (e.g., via YAML bindings).
Strategy Pattern
Lets the system choose an algorithm or provider dynamically.
Automatically selects the optimal AI vendor per context (cost, latency, region).
Decorator / Proxy Pattern
Wraps functions with added behavior without altering core logic.
Used in interceptors for enforcing RLS, PII masking, and telemetry.
Command Pattern
Encapsulates each workflow step as an executable command.
Ensures deterministic, resumable orchestration of multi-step workflows.
State Machine Pattern
Manages workflow progression across nodes (start, pause, fail, resume).
Ensures consistent execution and recovery — critical for compliance and reliability.
Repository Pattern
Standardized data access layer separating logic from storage.
Centralizes audit, telemetry, and state management for transparency.
Builder Pattern
Step-by-step creation of complex objects (here, YAML workflows).
Allows dynamic construction of executable workflows from DSL or user input.
DAG (Directed Acyclic Graph)
A network of connected workflow steps with no loops.
Ensures predictable, auditable task flow — no infinite loops or uncontrolled recursion.
Interceptor
Middleware that applies rules to every operation before it executes.
Enforces compliance policies universally across the system.
YAML (YAML Ain’t Markup Language)
Human-readable configuration format used for declarative control.
Lets non-engineers safely modify workflows and governance settings.
SOLID Principles
Software design philosophy emphasizing maintainability and clarity.
Ensures extensibility, prevents code rot, and keeps system logic reusable.
Port & Adapter Architecture (Hexagonal)
Separates business logic (core) from external dependencies.
Makes the platform adaptable to new tools, APIs, or data sources.
Config-as-Code
Managing logic and policies through configuration files.
Enables safe, versioned change management with full audit trail.
Executive Close
The bank isn’t just deploying AI — it’s building an AI control plane that can evolve faster than the industry changes.

By separating configuration, governance, and orchestration, this architecture ensures Northern Bank’s AI capabilities remain auditable, adaptable, and cloud-agnostic for the next decade — no matter how quickly the AI landscape shifts.


